/target
*.d

# ---- ROMs are build output, never source -----------------------------------
#
# Every .gba in this tree is produced by a `make *-rom` target and rewritten by
# agb-gbafix; none is input to anything, and none belongs in the repository.
# One broad rule, deliberately not a list of names: the list this replaces had
# grown to 13 exact filenames (one per ROM target, plus one per instrument
# image), so every new image — 6a-5, 6a-5b, 6a-7, E5, … — was one forgotten
# line away from committing a 1.9 MB binary to the repository root. A rule that
# has to be extended for each new build is a rule that will be missed.
#
# The intended shape is "no ROM, under any name, anywhere". If a .gba ever IS
# source — a committed reference image a test or a document reads — it belongs
# in a specific fixture directory, and that directory gets a scoped negation
# next to this rule (\`!\` re-includes, and a name pattern re-narrows it):
#
#     !/assets/reference/**/*.gba
#
# Do not re-open the root and do not add a filename here for a new target.
*.gba

# mGBA writes a save file beside the ROM on every local run
# (e.g. `gba-sound-player.sav` next to `gba-sound-player.gba`).
# Emulator state, never build output we want tracked.
*.sav

# macOS Finder metadata
.DS_Store

# Standalone build dir for the symphonia FLAC compile probe example
# (examples/symphonia_flac_probe/ has its own Cargo.toml/workspace)
/examples/symphonia_flac_probe/target/

# flac-lite is its own workspace (crates/flac-lite/Cargo.toml has [workspace]),
# so it keeps its own target/ — and it builds for BOTH thumbv4t-none-eabi and the
# host triple, so the paths vary; ignore the whole dir. The root `/target` rule
# above is anchored and does not cover it.
/crates/*/target/

# Same story for the experimental FLAC integration ROM crate (examples/flac_integration/
# is its own workspace, builds only for thumbv4t-none-eabi via `make native-flac-rom`).
/examples/flac_integration/target/

# And the perf-gate spike crate (examples/flac_spike/ — own workspace; builds
# for the GBA target via `make native-spike-rom` AND for the host triple for
# its witness tests, so the paths vary; ignore the whole dir). NOTE: its
# assets/*.bin + src/assets.rs are deliberately TRACKED — they are committed
# generator output, the witness the spike measures. Integer-synthesized PCM that
# regenerates byte-identically is why it is an *instrument*, which is the class
# ALBUM-PLAYER.md §2.3 keeps: instruments are tracked, per-album audio is build
# output and follows the master rule below.
/examples/flac_spike/target/

# Master-audio build artifacts (7-3): generated by `make master-pack` from
# `assets/sound/*.wav`; the tracked product asset is never overwritten. This is the
# division ALBUM-PLAYER.md §2.3 applies to the album build — the master is tracked as
# the instrument it is, its packed per-ROM forms are output — so the album's blobs,
# manifest and pin module land here too when Phase 1 adds them.
/examples/flac_integration/assets/master.gfp
/examples/flac_integration/src/master_pin.rs

# PR E's album corpus (the ULI2 tail witness): generated by `make
# uli-album-asset` from the tracked master at `assets/sound/`, and pinned by the
# C reference decoder. The blob, the f32 intermediate, the reference decode and
# the pin module are all output; `assets/sound/bioluminescent.wav` and
# `scripts/uli_album_pin.py` are the tracked input and recipe.
/examples/uli_bench/assets/
/examples/uli_bench/src/album_pin.rs
# E3's and PR D's corpora have their own generated pins, same rule.
/examples/uli_bench/src/album_pin_adv.rs
/examples/uli_bench/src/album_pin_cross.rs
/examples/uli_bench/src/album_pin_two.rs

# The sample-rate ROM flow (FLAC.md "Sample-rate reduction"): its generated
# clips + modules are TEMPORARY like ROMs — regenerate with
# `make rate-assets INPUT=… RATE=…`, removed by `make clean`. The committed
# spike assets above stay tracked; these never are. (Its `.gba` outputs are
# covered by the ROM rule at the top of this file.)
/examples/flac_spike/assets/rate_*.bin
/examples/flac_spike/src/rate_assets.rs
/examples/flac_spike/src/rate_pcm.rs

# The Opus spike crate (examples/opus_spike/ — own workspace, same pattern).
# Its vendored decoder is its OWN workspace root (vendor/opus-rs/Cargo.toml
# carries [workspace] — OPUS.md's standalone probe recipe runs from there),
# so it builds a target/ of its own; ignore both build dirs.
/examples/opus_spike/target/
/examples/opus_spike/vendor/opus-rs/target/

# The E10 ARM-kernels crate (examples/arm-kernels/ — own workspace, same
# pattern; builds for the GBA target AND the host triple, so the paths vary;
# ignore the whole dir. Its committed golden table src/vectors.rs is source,
# not build output — tracked).
/examples/arm-kernels/target/

# The vendored decoder's committed lockfile pins the exact dependency graph
# the thumbv4t gate builds with (flac_spike tracks its Cargo.lock — same rule).
!/examples/opus_spike/Cargo.lock

# Python bytecode caches (scripts/*.py); never commit.
__pycache__/

# The 6a-4 kernel-check ROM crate (examples/overlay-kernel-check/ — own
# workspace, same pattern; builds a target/ of its own, and the ROM it fixes is
# build output like every other one).
/examples/overlay-kernel-check/target/
/examples/e5_fetch/target/

# The ULI2 Phase 1b cycle harness (examples/uli_bench/ — own workspace, same
# pattern). Its Cargo.lock is committed: the instrument's dependency graph is
# part of the measurement.
/examples/uli_bench/target/
!/examples/uli_bench/Cargo.lock

# Cycle-harness logs are per-run output; the numbers that matter live in
# ULI2.md, and `make uli-bench` can regenerate the raw log. PR E adds two runs on
# the album corpus — the decode wall (`uli-album-bench`, whose log name the rule
# below already catches) and the gate total (`uli-gate`).
/uli-bench*.log
/uli-album-bench*.log
/uli-gate*.log
/uli-gate-fixture*.log
/uli-player*.log
/uli-playback-test.log
/uli-cross-gate*.log
/uli-adv-*.log

# The PR D player crate and its host suite (own workspace, builds for both the
# GBA target and the host triple). Its lockfile is part of the instrument, like
# uli_bench's.
/examples/uli_player/target/
!/examples/uli_player/Cargo.lock

# Timing-only by-difference attribution arms (`make uli-attrib`), and the
# sub-frame entropy arms below the frame level (`make uli-attrib-subframe`).
/uli-attrib*.log
/uli-subframe*.log
/uli-kernel-twin*.log

# The Album Player Phase 0 probe crate (examples/album_probe/ — own workspace,
# same pattern; builds for the GBA target and for the host triple, so the paths
# vary). Its assets/probe_art1.bin and src/art_pin.rs are deliberately TRACKED —
# committed generator output is the witness the AP-0 host gate reads, so the blob
# is the evidence the script ran, not a build artifact. That puts it in §2.3's
# instrument class (a host gate whose job is these exact bytes, from a deterministic
# in-tree generator), which is no kind of precedent for committing per-album audio.
# Its Cargo.lock is committed like uli_bench's: the probe's dependency graph is part
# of the record.
/examples/album_probe/target/
!/examples/album_probe/Cargo.lock

# AP-0's per-run gate/ROM logs are output; `make album-probe-*` rewrites them and
# the numbers that matter live in the probe log.
/album-probe*.log

# `make playback-test`'s log (crates/gba-playback's host gate) is the same shape:
# per-run output, rewritten on every run, with the counts recorded per landing in
# flac-docs/measurements.md rather than here.
/playback-test.log

# The Album Player's generated paths (ALBUM-PLAYER.md §2.3, §9.0). This is the
# directory the tracked `album.toml` is the ONLY source member of: everything the
# bake produces is output, so a reviewer reads the config and the pack report, and
# a fresh clone regenerates the bytes with `make album-bake`. The rule is per path
# with its reason, not a list of filenames that has to grow every phase.
#
# `target/` — the crate is its own cargo workspace (builds for the host triple
# here, and for the GBA once PR-3 adds a ROM main), so it keeps its own target dir.
/examples/album_player/target/
# The committed lockfile pins this crate's (currently empty) dependency graph the
# way album_probe's and uli_bench's do — part of the record, not build output.
!/examples/album_player/Cargo.lock
# The whole assets dir — `albm1.bin` and every per-track `trackNN.gfp` — is
# generated by `make album-bake` from `album.toml`. Never tracked: per-album audio
# is the class §2.3 keeps out of the tree, and the master it is derived from is
# already tracked as the instrument it is (the `master.gfp` rule above).
/examples/album_player/assets/
# The generated `assets` pin module, `mod`-declared by `src/lib.rs`. A fresh clone
# has none; `make check` formats the crate through
# scripts/fmt_with_generated_stub.sh's GENERATED override, exactly as
# flac_integration's master pin is handled above.
/examples/album_player/src/assets.rs
# The generated `layout` module (2a PR-1, §5/§9.0's D9 row): the geometry, bar grid and
# derived face figures of the variant `[layout]` selects. Never tracked, for the same two
# reasons as the pin module above — it is a function of tracked inputs (`layout_spec.toml`
# and `album.toml`, both tracked), and tracking it would put a second copy of every derived
# number in the tree for a reviewer to diff against the spec instead of reading the spec.
# §9.0's duty (i) is what makes it safe: the bake re-reads the module it wrote and refuses
# a grid disagreement, and the ROM's boot check (`meta.rs`) refuses a manifest that disagrees
# with the module linked into it — R11's stale-artifact failure, caught at boot rather than
# in a host test that shares the artifact. Same fmt stub as assets.rs, same `make clean`.
/examples/album_player/src/layout.rs
# 2b PR-(1)'s machine-readable layout report: the same derivation as the bake's prose
# audit, as sorted `key=value` (per-scene kind/resident/own tiles, the resident-set pool
# split, the ROM addends and the labelled cycle terms). Build output like the modules:
# written by the bake, a function of the tracked spec, and `make album-layout-test` holds
# it byte-identical across two emits rather than tracking a copy to diff.
/examples/album_player/layout_report.txt
# The generated `glyphs` module (2a PR-2, §5.5a step 5): the glyph atlas — the ASCII cut
# of the ONE face the selected variant's text elements declare, walked out of that tracked
# `.ttf` at the raster the spec declares. Never tracked, on the same two grounds as
# layout.rs above: it is a function of tracked inputs (`layout_spec.toml`, the face file,
# and `pyftsubset`, whose version §5.5a makes part of the record), and tracking it would
# put 20 KB of glyph bytes in the diff for every change to a face — the exact reason the
# subset source itself is not tracked either (§5.5a step 1). §9.0 duty (i) covers it the
# same way: `make album-bake` regenerates it, `make album-glyphs-test` compares the bytes
# on disk against a fresh generation, and the module's own `const _: ()` asserts refuse to
# compile if it disagrees with the `layout` module generated beside it.
/examples/album_player/src/glyphs.rs
# `make album-glyphs-test`'s log (2a PR-2): the atlas tool's legs and the sources they
# name, rewritten every run — the per-run rule as above.
/album-glyphs-test.log
# `make album-pack-check`'s log: per-run output, rewritten every run, with the
# counts recorded per landing elsewhere (the playback-test.log/album-probe*.log rule).
/album-pack-check.log
# `make album-seam-test`'s log: the same per-run rule as the pack gate's above.
/album-seam-test.log
# `make album-layout-test`'s log (2a): the self-test's legs and the sources they name,
# rewritten every run. The counts that matter are recorded in the commit that changes
# them, not here.
/album-layout-test.log
# `make album-preview`'s pictures (2a PR-3): the surface rendered at 1x and 4x, plus the
# character grid written beside each. An instrument for a human, regenerated on demand —
# the same class as the ROMs above, and not a gate artifact.
/album-preview-*.png
/album-preview-*.png.txt
# `make album-rom`'s headless run log: the same per-run rule again. The run is the
# record (`[album-rom] verdict`), never a committed artifact.
/album-rom-run.log
# `make album-test`'s headless run log (AP-1's gate): the same per-run rule once more,
# and the one the gate itself reads back, so it is output in both senses.
/album-test.log
# `make album-card-test`'s headless run log (2b PR-(3)): the gate script plus one Select
# edge, read back by the target that wrote it — the same per-run rule.
/album-card-test.log
